// in ../js/main.mjs.
//
// A hand-written recursive-descent parser (AWFY's JsonPureStringParser) parses the RapBenchmark
// constant into JsonObject/JsonArray/JsonNumber/JsonString/JsonLiteral values; no std JSON
// code is used. run(n) parses it n times and adds, per parse, the size of the `operations`
// array that AWFY's verifyResult checks (156), or 0 when a check fails. Sakura Lang has no
// mutable objects, so the parser's state is a record every parser function takes and returns,
// and differences from AWFY are:
// - characters: AWFY reads `input.substring(index, index + 1)` per character. A Sakura String
//   has no O(1) character access (String.slice takes byte offsets and is O(length) on JS), so
//   the Parser converts its input once with String.to_bytes (O(n), per parse, as AWFY's
//   constructor takes the String) and reads Bytes.get(input, index) -> Option<Int>; `current`
//   is that byte, -1 for AWFY's null, compared with character codes (Sakura has no character
//   literals; each code is commented). Captures decode Bytes.slice with String.from_bytes.
// - SOM's Vector is a List grown with List.push (append), List.get (at) and List.len (size);
//   values are an enum and JsonLiteral's three singletons are the cases of Literal.
// - HashIndexTable: Sakura has no bitwise operators, so `& (length - 1)` is `% length` and
//   `& 0xff` is `% 256`; both agree on the nonnegative values they see.
// - a parse error is a defect carrying AWFY's message; JsonObject.get returns None for AWFY's
//   null, which run(n) counts as a failed check.
